starlark 0.14.2 as Cadpole builds it: scientific float output keeps its
rounding carry.

write_scientific (behind str(), repr(), print, "%s", "%g", "%e" and
"{}".format() of any float whose exponent is >= 6 or <= -6) wrote the
leading digit as normal.trunc() and then rounded the six digits after it
on their own. When the fraction was >= 0.9999995 the tail rounded to
1000000, the digit loop emitted zeros, and the carry into the leading
digit (and, for 9.9999997, into the exponent) was dropped: 1999999.7
printed as 1e+06, 9999999.7 as 9e+06. Volumes in mm^3 of round-number
parts land there routinely after a rotate or boolean.

The fix formats the magnitude with Rust's `{:.6e}`, which is an exact,
correctly rounded decimal conversion with the carry, and keeps the
crate's own conventions: a leading "-" for a negative number, trailing
zeros stripped when asked, and a signed two-digit exponent. Applied by
crate.annotation in MODULE.bazel; drop it when an upstream starlark
release fixes write_scientific.

